  "title": "A plain crossing inside braces",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Ladders of plain cube junctions cross wires exactly after interpolation, inside braces. A crossing site whose wire A runs from its point end and wire B from its line end, with a start rung and a rung between consecutive junctions (no caps), acts by (NW)^k, so the exact crossing is the identity, the k = 0 term. For two plain cubes at a square, NW has characteristic polynomial (x^2 - 19x - 1)(x^2 + 7x + 13): a unit of norm -1 in Q(sqrt 365) and the two primes above 13 in Z[omega], so the squared eigenvalue products are distinct for distinct count vectors of equal total (the lattice condition). #col(K3,3) = 6 is recovered from plain instances with 1, 3, 5, 7 junctions (20 to 104 vertices), each cubic bipartite, certified Pfaffian, cyclically 4-edge-connected (a brace), and twisted on all three branches at every junction.",
